Date: Sun, 31 Oct 93 15:03:20 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

Jamie Dinkelacker on what constitutes a great startup:
>> * Management talks about the next 90-180 days, not after the singularlity;
>> * The company has at least one "visionary" in the top ranks;
>> * Efficient level of secretarial/administrative support;
>> * Cooperative (at least tolerant) attitudes between marketing and
>> engineering/development;
>> * The company has a clear picture of who their customer is;
>> * Low debt structure (aside from the Venture game and its nuances).

Peter Bastien:
> how to quantify these investing heuristics and to prove or disprove
> if such heuristics are sufficient. 

That's one way to go, but many of these are very subjective and 
information-rich, not the kinds of things that can be compressed 
into expert system rules.  But the exceptions could be important:
level of secretarial support can be put into a software model
(if the data is available), and debt structure data is usually
available.  Other such data might include number of PhDs on staff,
years of experience of the founders in their fields, etc.  Alas,
even with the seemingly straightforward data, by compressing human
knowledge to numbers we lose some exception-handling (eg the
nuances of the Venture game if that's involved).  

Another way to go is to have those with unique rich or subjective 
information about these companies continually rate them in various 
areas, then feed those ratings to the software.

The market already constitutes a very lossy compression,
so losing such nuances probably doesn't wreck the model --
but such distortion should be minimized.

> 	The most difficult part would be to prove or disprove if these
> heuristics are sufficient. 

That's where we bring in Kolmogorov, statistics, etc. (cf. that part 
of the thread) and lots of market data.  Mostly likely, such
rules by themselves are insufficient by the time they are compressed
into software. Much better to incorporate the rules into software 
that can than also create its own rules, and evolve the mix into
an optimal strategy, again using statistical and parsimony tests.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

