John K Clark says in X-Message-Number: #93-10-999:
>		>>Hans Moravec calls these ideas "Universal Realization" and
>		>>is currently writing a book about it. His main points:          
>		>>1) Anything can simulate anything.
		
>	>This has to be wrong, [...]  "We're about to run a simulation
>	>of Albert Einstein using this blade of grass and a wet blanket." 

>Grass and wet blankets, why not?  [...] a computer [...]
>Weizenbaum showed how to make one using a roll of toilet paper
>and a pile of small stones. Just make sure the computer is big
>enough, run the correct program and "it's a honor to meet you
>Professor Einstein ".
	
Unfortunately we couldn't ever resurrect Einstein even with the most
superb nanocomputers of post-Singularity.  As far as I know he wasn't
frozen and didn't have his synapses analysed and recorded.  

I believe Hans Moravec is in favour of trying to resurrect people from
their writings.  In the future it should be a trivial task to generate
_an_ identity from a bunch of written material and then upload it, but
the individual that you'd have would be quite distinct from _the_ original.
On the grounds that even a distant twin is better than nothing, I'd
recommend that we do this for those Extropians who fail to make it past the
Singularity for some reason.  The archives of their posts should still be
available (we'd need to make a list of who doesn't want this facility.)  In
intermediate cases, an individual might get cryonically suspended in much
less than ideal circumstances, and it turns out there are still a good deal
of unknown variables when it comes to defining their identity.  Rather than
guess, written material could resolve much of the data.

